#RESEARCHES
research.KT_ANCIENTS.title=The Lost Knowledge
research.KT_ANCIENTS.title.subtitle=[KMT] Is it future or is it past?

research.KT_ANCIENTS.text.stage.1=Sometimes it seems to me that there are things which elude even my extraordinary mind. Ever since I encountered the reified void - a phenomenon I still do not fully understand - a thought has been visiting me. A thought that seems to surpass any madness I have experienced thus far.<BR>Is it possible that the ancient revelations I have relied upon until now... are merely a kind of haze? An illusion, a cunning ruse designed to distract me from something far more important?<BR>This knowledge is certainly impressive. It intrigues and shocks. Yet, the price one must pay for it often proves too high. It is as if the Void itself is playing shells with me.<BR>In search of answers, I have explored the darkest corners of the world. I have peered into its depths, journeyed through the Nether, the End, the Outer Lands... and found nothing. Nothing I had not encountered before.<BR>I might have thought I had lost some faculties due to prolonged exposure to the Warp, yet it seems to me that is not the case. The tools I rely on are still quite primitive. I managed to reach new heights in working with the gauntlet... perhaps I can do the same with the Thaumometer?
research.KT_ANCIENTS.text.stage.2=This device... I do not know whether to deem it a success or a failure.<BR>While further investigating the properties of Impetus, I pondered how its gravitational nature might distort light rays passing through the lens. And given the Thaumomometer's properties - it might even influence the information itself in a way, revealing things previously unseen.<BR>Yet, I have not encountered a single object that was affected in any other manner.<BR>The only effect I have discovered is utterly horrific: by carrying a gauntlet equipped with a charged Impetus Conductor, I can aim the Thaumometer's lens at any living creature I choose and pierce it with my gaze, causing it to start crumbling into pieces.<BR>This is not what I seek. There must be something more. Something out in the Outer Lands.
research.KT_ANCIENTS.text.stage.3=Keys! Keys from the labyrinth. The Thaumometer revealed inscriptions on them that I couldn't decipher without it. And the most surprising thing is the language. I... understand it? But what truly alarms me is their content. There is no coherent text here, only fragments, and even within those, some details seem irretrievably lost. Yet, the parts I *can* read shed light on this world's past. And if these scratched artifacts aren't lying, the reality is far more terrifying than I could have imagined. I need to venture deeper into the ancient structures. I can't keep the keys themselves—they are needed to open the doors—but I can make impressions of them using a blank seal. To do this, I simply need to combine the seal with a revealed key in the crafting grid, and then take care not to lose it. Once I’ve gathered enough, I might finally be able to piece the puzzle together. And I already know who can help me with that.
research.KT_ANCIENTS.text.stage.4=When the infusion was complete, I felt the air around me tremble and my hands shake. Had I really succeeded? Had I truly accomplished something great, and not fallen into yet another of the same delusions I'd repeatedly plucked from the void?<BR>I immediately grabbed the red tablet of hard wax and began to read its contents, not even reaching the research table. The text was written in several layers, words were often repeated, some were crossed out, and others were missing again, but my mind was crystal clear. I understood everything that was here...<BR>...<BR>No, this can't be...<BR>...<BR>Catastrophic mistake! Insane misinterpretation!<BR>Now I understand. The text on the tablet blinds me, passing through my eyes, burning the very connections in my brain, but I understand.<BR>Those beings I called Ancients never had anything to do with them. These mindless phantoms, rotting carcasses, delirious visions... Of course! How foolish was I to think so! Could they have collected libraries, studied and built altars, raised spires to the heavens, and, finally, resisted the void?<BR>Resisted, indeed. I doubted it before, but now I have irrefutable proof that the Outer Lands were destroyed by war. Although... no. The tablet's account speaks more of *total annihilation*. Constant ruptures of the void created this imitation of life, Eldritch, in the places where they touched matter. Waves of black-violet swarms engulfed the world until it was completely dismembered and digested.<BR>The Ancients... the data I've collected is scant on their inventions. However, I can say with certainty that the materials from which the keys are made are absolutely unique and have never been encountered before. And yet, there is something very familiar about this orange glow. Familiar, yet long forgotten...

research.KT_BASE.title=Further Zenith Studies
research.KT_BASE.title.subtitle=[KMT] Beyond the limit of beyond the limit
research.KT_BASE.text.stage.1=Ever since I crafted the Awakened Ichorium, I believed I had reached the apogee - that there was a definitive limit, a maximum beyond which further progress was simply impossible. <BR>But now, with the discovery of Ancient materials, I see a gap in my research.<BR>When deriving the Ichor formula, I relied on brute force. Nether Stars and Primal Pearls are incredibly potent when used in tandem; that is precisely what led me to the mistaken belief that I had reached the terminal limit.<BR>Yet, the engineering potential of this armor has not been fully realized. Imagine what more could be achieved if every single detail were meticulously refined with new technology in mind.

research.KT_ICHORFLAME.title=Ichorflame
research.KT_ICHORFLAME.title.subtitle=[KMT] A rather expensive toy

research.KT_ICHORFLAME.text.stage.1=While studying the properties of the substance I call Ichor, I noticed an interesting feature that had previously escaped my attention.<BR>The Ichor's energy potential is immense, yet the balance of aspects within it prevents the substance from heating up and detonating in my pocket.<BR>On the other hand, disrupting it is quite simple - and I already have a theory on how to achieve that. The main thing is to ensure my own safety.
research.KT_ICHORFLAME.text.stage.2=This isn't quite the result I was counting on. I did manage to shift the balance somewhat, but... what's this? A Nitor?<BR>Well... looks like not every product of thaumaturgy has to defy the laws of physics or drive one mad.<BR>Be that it is may, now I have a massive flame - twice the size of ordinary Nitor, shimmering with seemingly infinite amount of colors.

research.KT_ICHORIUMNEEDLE.title=Ichorium Needle of the Voidbanisher
research.KT_ICHORIUMNEEDLE.title.subtitle=[KMT] No more than a mosquito bite

research.KT_ICHORIUMNEEDLE.text.stage.1=Although Ichor armor renders me almost immune to any external irritants, this world still does contain threats that could cause me considerable trouble.<BR>The very occult nature of Thaumaturgy contributes to this; one cannot always predict what godforsaken monstrosity might emerge from a given experiment.<BR>Clearly, for a researcher of my caliber, wasting one's nerves on trifles is simply undignified. At times, I wish I had something at my disposal capable of banishing an assailant to the darkest corner of reality—a place from which they would most certainly never return.
research.KT_ICHORIUMNEEDLE.text.stage.2=A tiny needle crafted from bone coated in Ichor. It turns out this material can not only warp local space but also transport living beings to almost anywhere in the world - even when fashioned into such crude tools.<BR>This "Needle of the Voidbanisher" possesses a specific property: upon striking any living target, it instantly hurls it into the space beneath the Overworld - far enough away for the Void to tear them apart immediately.<BR>Clearly, this cannot be considered an ultimate weapon; at my level of progression, opponents are valued primarily for the resources they yield, and the needle prevents that. Then again, it has a different purpose - one it fulfills admirably.<BR>I forgot to mention: despite being made of Ichor, the item breaks upon use. Or rather... it vanishes into the Void along with the target. Oops!

research.KT_CRYSTALCLUSTER.title=Crystal Purification

research.KT_CRYSTALCLUSTER.text.stage.1=The crystals found in the Outer Lands are a vital and valuable resource for the most promising research.<BR>However, the fact that this "ore" does not occur in the Overworld makes hunting for it quite difficult - difficult enough that a 1:1 conversion rate feels like highway robbery.<BR>I have applied the metal cluster creation principle I already know to produce a cluster of this strange crystal. The cost in Aspects is higher, and I require *Vitreous* instead of *Metallum*, but it should be worth it.

research.KT_ENERGYBRANCH.title=Energy Potential
research.KT_ENERGYBRANCH.text.stage.1=This branch of research will focus on the possibility of modifying ichor to enable the direct utilization of the energy from the primordial pearls contained within it.<BR>It also encompasses any theories involving the use of ichor as a working fluid in various devices.

research.KT_MATERIALBRANCH.title=Structural Properties
research.KT_MATERIALBRANCH.text.stage.1=This branch of research will focus on the properties of ichor itself as a structural material, as well as its potential applications - including the creation of alloys, composites, electronic components, and advanced protective plating for heavy-duty armor.

research.KT_SPACEBRANCH.title=Spatial Effects
research.KT_SPACEBRANCH.text.stage.1=This branch of research will focus on the interaction between ichor and objects that compromise the integrity of reality - such as void rifts - as well as its inherent ability to warp space.

research.KT_ROOTCRYSTAL.title=Growing Bedrock
research.KT_ROOTCRYSTAL.title.subtitle=[KMT] As below, so above
research.KT_ROOTCRYSTAL.text.stage.1=The longer I study Ichor and the notes of obscure authorship that appear here and there in the Thaumonomicon, the more convinced I become of two maxims:<BR>First, the blood of the gods had already been discovered and studied in the past. The credit goes to those Thaumaturges who pioneered this discipline and made the crucial breakthroughs that ultimately led to our modern understanding of most worldly processes.<BR>Second, my formula differs somewhat from the one used to create the items described on the pages I discovered.<BR>One of the most regrettable losses was the ability to shatter bedrock and gain access to that—well... "place" beyond it.<BR>Anyway, these thoughts lead to an idea: since I cannot break the bedrock directly, perhaps I can find a way to get my hands on a fragment of it by other means?
research.KT_ROOTCRYSTAL.text.stage.2=Things took a much more interesting turn.<BR>Ichor reacts vigorously to any crystalline structures - such as diamond - by spontaneously forming its own crystals around them.<BR>I simply needed to add a Void Seed to bring the material's quality closer to that of Bedrock, and a Pure Shard to lock the crystal in an inert state.<BR>Once prepared, the crystal can be "sprouted" by tossing it into a crucible. I deliberately split the process into two stages, as this allowed for a significantly higher yield using the same amount components.

research.KT_PERSISTENCE.title=Seal of Persistence
research.KT_PERSISTENCE.title.subtitle=[KTM] Hands off!
research.KT_PERSISTENCE.text.stage.1=Discovering that Ichor could be used to reinforce armor and weapons made from other materials marked a significant milestone in my research. Yet, I cannot say I am entirely satisfied with the result.<BR>It feels somewhat... unconvincing. Yes, technically, the items become indestructible when used for their intended purpose; no matter how carelessly I handle them or how hard the rock I’m breaking, the material holds firm.<BR>But what happens if the weapon slips from my grasp? What if it gets knocked out, or I drop it - or if I were to die?<BR>No, the worst-case scenario isn't losing a priceless piece of gear to fire or an explosion... the worst thing would be for someone else to get their hands on it. For someone to dare touch the very thing I have strived so long to create.
research.KT_PERSISTENCE.text.stage.2=I already had all the necessary substances and their properties at hand; the only question was finding the right way to combine them.<BR>I discovered another intriguing trait of Primal Pearls: they can literally melt down imbued foci, effectively embedding the spell structure directly into the item being infused. The result is something akin to an enchantment, but far more stable and permanent.<BR>The only issue is that the concentration of such potent magic places a heavy strain on the operator's psyche. Simply put, carrying items infused with foci would cause me to slowly but inevitably lose my mind.<BR>Anyway, the recipe I’ve noted on this page yields the so-called Seal of Persistence. To use it, I must hold any non-block item in my off-hand and hold down the right mouse button while the seal is in my main hand.<BR>This process also requires me to have a Totem of Undying in my inventory. And where am I supposed to get them? Well, Pechs might finally come in handy after all...<BR>An item protected with Seal of Persistence cannot be blown up, burned, or otherwise destroyed. It will not dissappear from the world like items left lying around for too long. Furthermore, anyone other than myself who attempts to pick it up will be brutally blinded and burned alive.

